<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Exarchate of Ravenna</h1><p class="st-meta">584 CE – 751 CE (approx.) · capital Ravenna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Byzantine province ruling what remained of imperial Italy from Ravenna, under an exarch combining military and civil power, until the Lombards took Ravenna in 751.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> invasion of 568 broke Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> into scattered territories, Emperor Maurice concentrated civil and military authority in a single governor, the exarch, based at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-ravenna">Ravenna</a>. The office is first attested in 584. It was a new form of government designed for a frontier under constant attack.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The exarch governed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-ravenna">Ravenna</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pentapolis-italy">Pentapolis</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-rome">Duchy of Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a> and other fragments, commanding local garrisons whose officers became a landed aristocracy. Relations with the popes were often tense, especially over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monothelitism">Monothelitism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-iconoclasm">Iconoclasm</a>. Several exarchs rebelled, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleutherius-exarch">Eleutherius</a> in 619 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympius-exarch">Olympius</a> in 649. Ravenna remained a centre of Greek-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> culture, its churches adorned with mosaics.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> pressure grew in the 8th century; King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aistulf">Aistulf</a> captured <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-ravenna">Ravenna</a> in 751, killing or expelling the last exarch, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eutychius-exarch">Eutychius</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a> defeated Aistulf, he gave the exarchate's lands to the pope in 756, forming the core of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/exarchate-of-ravenna.jpg" alt="Exarchate of Ravenna" width="320" height="249"><figcaption>Image: Shuaaa2 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Exarchate_of_Italy_-_600_AD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>584 CE – 751 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ravenna</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Smaragdus, Isaac, Eutychius</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chalcedonian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Italy</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Exarchate of Italy</dd></dl></aside></div>

